  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nA quiet start is in store over the long term for our area with a \ncontinuation of the sunny and dry conditions through the end of the \nupcoming work week. At the mid and upper levels, conditions remain \ndry, with low RH values associated with a mid-level ridge that will \nbreak down by the end of the week. At the surface, a regional high \npressure centered over the mid-Atlantic will weaken by mid-week, and \nwill be absorbed into stronger surface high pressure transiting the \nnortheast coast. Ultimately, this setup will continue to support a \ngeneral northeast wind component channeled along the lee side of the \nAppalachians into our area through the end of the week.\n\nWhile we will have slightly moister low levels due to the northeast \nto east component of our surface winds, some dry air and lower RH \nvalues will filter over our area by Tuesday, so we will keep an eye \non the need to issue a fire danger statement on this day. Otherwise, \ntemperatures will gradually climb each day through the week, with \ndaytime highs rising from the low to upper 70s up into the low 80s \nover central Georgia by Friday into Halloween weekend. \n\nLooking ahead to next weekend, guidance suggests a shift in the \nsynoptic pattern becoming more zonally oriented by the end of the \nweek. As far as precipitation is concerned, rain chances do look to \nbe on the increase by the end of the long term, but models are not \nin agreement with the development or placement of the surface low \npressure. As a result, whether we see any precipitation or the \nextent of coverage will need to be fine tuned as we progress over \nthe next few days. At this time, precipitation chances are still low \nfor Halloween night.",
